00:55:00,000 --> 00:55:06,000
Lethal Weapon is a 1987 American action thriller film directed
by Richard Donner and written by Shane Black.

11
01:00:30,000 --> 01:00:36,000
Starring Mel Gibson and Danny Glover, the film follows two mismatched LAPD detectives—Martin Riggs (Gibson), a volatile former Special Forces soldier struggling with suicidal impulses after his wife's
death, and Roger Murtaugh (Glover), a seasoned homicide sergeant and devoted family man—who are partnered to investigate a young woman's apparent suicide during the Christmas season.

12
01:06:00,000 --> 01:06:06,000
The supporting cast includes Gary Busey, Tom
Atkins, Darlene Love, and Mitchell Ryan.

13
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:36,000
Conceived by then-unknown screenwriter Shane Black as a contemporary urban Western exploring trauma, masculinity, and moral decay in Los Angeles, the film
underwent extensive development under producer Joel Silver and was shaped by Donner's emphasis on character dynamics and gallows humor.

14
01:17:00,000 --> 01:17:06,000
Black's script, among the highest-selling spec scripts of the
1980s, helped redefine the buddy cop genre.

15
01:22:30,000 --> 01:22:36,000
Released theatrically in the United States on March 6, 1987, by Warner Bros., Lethal Weapon was both a critical and commercial success,
grossing over $120 million worldwide against a $15 million budget and earning an Academy Award nomination for Best Sound.

16
01:28:00,000 --> 01:28:06,000
Its success established Gibson and Glover as major stars and solidified Donner's
reputation for high-energy, character-driven action filmmaking.

17
01:33:30,000 --> 01:33:36,000
Lethal Weapon spawned a media franchise that includes three sequels,
beginning with Lethal Weapon 2 (1989), and a television adaptation.

18
01:39:00,000 --> 01:39:06,000
The film explores themes of showdown, police, self-destruction,
mixed martial arts (mma), los angeles, california.

19
01:44:30,000 --> 01:44:36,000
The tone is lighthearted and intense.
